   "t": 68.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b5/3NP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "Nxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Essentially a Scotch where we've skipped the knight to c6 — and our bishop already presses his knight."
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 143,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b5/3NP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQ - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "The most ambitious choice is to castle: flexible, and the pawn on e4 is weak — if he dawdles one more move we pile up on it with the rook. Even e5 is never dangerous; we just answer with the rook to e8."
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 234,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b5/3NP3/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R1BQK2R b KQ - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Be2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop to e2 is a touch passive — nothing wrong with it, but it hands us the initiative."
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 263,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b5/3NP3/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R1BQK2R w KQ - 3 7",
   "line": [
    "Re8"
   ],
   "spoken": "The rook to e8, attacking the pawn; if he props it with f3, that's a huge concession — it weakens the diagonal and we strike with d5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 272,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b3B1/3NP3/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 4 7",
   "line": [
    "Bg5"
   ],
   "spoken": "He tries to defend the pawn indirectly by pinning the knight."
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 283,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1pp1/5n1p/2b3B1/3NP3/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "h6"
   ],
   "spoken": "So we chase the bishop away — and if it takes the knight, that's a fine trade for us."
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 286.5,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1pp1/5n1p/2b5/3NP2B/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 1 8",
   "line": [
    "Bh4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 350.5,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/5n1p/2b3p1/3NP2B/2N5/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "g5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Should we play g5? It wins the pawn but weakens the king — and concretely it's worth it: he must retreat, we take on e4, his knight on d4 is left hanging with his king still uncastled, and we consolidate in the time he needs to untangle."
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 352.5,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/5n1p/2b3p1/3NP3/2N3B1/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Bg3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 377,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/7p/2b3p1/3Nn3/2N3B1/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "Nxe4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight takes on e4 — and now the jump to f5 is available, because the bishop still doesn't cover that square; we haven't committed the d-pawn."
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 399,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/7p/2b2Np1/4n3/2N3B1/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 1 10",
   "line": [
    "Nf5"
   ],
   "spoken": "He jumps to f5 at once. Taking his bishop would be bad — the recapture opens his rook — so instead we look at taking the knight."
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 476,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/7p/2b2Np1/8/2n3B1/PPP1BPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Nxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight takes on c3 — a stabilising move; if the queen checks on d3, we simply take the knight anyway, and then the queen comes to f6."
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 486.5,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pppp1p2/7p/2b2Np1/8/2P3B1/P1P1BPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 0 11",
